# Conversion strategies

Every strategy is only a per-year dollar list. The engine still draws from
tax-deferred accounts, taxes the conversion, and deposits Roth. Strategies
do not change income, expenses, withdrawals, or RMD rules.

## Strategies

| Value | What it schedules |
| ---- | ---- |
| `even` | Convertible total (or `convertAmount`) spread across the pre-RMD window. |
| `immediate` | Same target in year 1. Engine caps at that year's live pre-RMD balance. |
| `fillBracket` | Each year, converts up to just under the top of `targetBracketRate` (12% / 22% / 24%; default 22%) and never past it. Starts from the room in the no-conversion projection, then re-projects with the schedule and settles each year onto the ceiling, since a conversion can phase out the senior deduction or make more Social Security taxable (overshoot), or be partly absorbed by unused deductions (undershoot). |
| `irmaa` | Each year, room just under the next IRMAA MAGI floor above baseline MAGI. Top tier: no MAGI cap. |
| `depleteByRmd` | Walk balances (grow, withdraw, convert `remaining / yearsLeft`, then deposits) so tax-deferred empties by RMD. |
| `minTax` | Lowest lifetime income tax: federal + state paid over the plan, the same `taxesTotal` the results show as Lifetime taxes. Tax on tax-deferred money left at the end is not counted. Starts from the best of no conversion and every other strategy, then nudges each conversion year up or down (step halves to $500) keeping changes that lower that total. Ignores Medicare and inheritance size. |
| `manual` | User-edited `manualSchedule`. Default for new plans. Shown first as Manual. Editing a year in the Conversion wizard (or on even/immediate) switches here. |

`buildConversionSchedule(household, refs)` is the switch. Fill-bracket and
IRMAA need live federal / Medicare tables via `refs`.

## Monthly shortfall limit

`optimizer.maxMonthlyShortfall` (missing = off) caps every strategy, Manual
included. `buildConversionSchedule` runs the strategy, then
`capConversionsToShortfall` (`src/lib/optimizer/shortfall.ts`) walks the years
in order and bisects each year's amount down until that year's `surplus`
(monthly) is at least `-maxMonthlyShortfall`. A year already short by more than
the limit with no conversion converts nothing. The menu's switch next to the
Conversion strategy heading sets it to `DEFAULT_MAX_MONTHLY_SHORTFALL` ($1,000)
or clears it. Strategy scores and Minimum taxes go through the same cap
(Minimum taxes searches uncapped, then is capped).

## Conversion tax: paid from income or assets

`optimizer.conversionTaxPaidFrom` (missing = `income`). Switch sits next to
the results tabs (`ConversionTaxToggle` in `ResultsView.tsx`).

- `income`: the year's tax lowers net income and surplus, as always.
- `assets`: in `projectScenario` step 5b, the tax the conversion adds (the
  year's tax minus the same year priced without the conversion) is withheld
  from the converted dollars. The Roth gets the rest, `annualTax` is
  unchanged, and `netMonthlyIncome` / `surplus` don't pay it. The row carries
  `conversionTaxWithheld`, shown as its own Tax paid from conversion section (monthly and annual) under Cash flow on Main, and a yearly line in Combined taxes on the detail table.
- The shortfall cap reads `surplus`, so it allows bigger conversions under
  `assets`. Surplus isn't banked into any account, so `assets` shows a lower
  inheritance than `income` for the same schedule.
- Rental passive-loss figures aren't re-priced for the no-conversion pass.

## Years before retirement are manual only (user rule)

No strategy converts before the first retirement year
(`retirementStartYear`), even when the projection starts earlier
("Projection starts" on the Household step). `strategyStartIndex` in
`optimizer/util.ts` is that year's index. Every strategy leaves the years
before it at 0 (depleteByRmd still grows and draws there), minTax never
searches them, the default even split and Spread evenly start at it, and
`buildConversionSchedule` clears them for any strategy but manual as a final
guard. Only amounts the user typed (a manual schedule, or the early
conversion rows) convert there. Tests: "years before retirement are manual
only" in `strategies.test.ts`.

Typed years: every year from today up to `strategyStartYear` (retirement,
or the plan start if later) is a typed row on the Conversion step, stored in
`optimizer.earlyConversions` whether it falls before the plan start or
inside it. `buildConversionSchedule` (`fitTypedConversions`) puts the
in-plan ones into the schedule (they win over a manual value there). When
typed amounts plus the strategy's exceed `convertibleTotal`, the strategy's
amounts are lowered from the latest year back; lowering keeps each year
inside its strategy's limit. Manual schedules and `allowOverConvertible`
plans are left as entered. Tests: "typed pre-retirement conversions over the
convertible total" in `strategies.test.ts`.

## Conversions before the plan starts (early conversions)

`optimizer.earlyConversions` (`{ year, amount }[]`, optional, one per year)
are extra conversions in the years from today up to the projection start,
e.g. 2026 and 2027 while still working. The user asked that they leave every
strategy and number as it was, so:

- **Manual only (user rule).** No strategy, Spread evenly, or default ever
  fills the years before the plan start. They hold only amounts the user
  typed. The engine test "is never filled by a strategy" pins this.
- Strategies, their schedules, the baseline, and the projection start don't
  read them. Only the Roth scenario does, through `runRothScenario` in
  `runScenario.ts` (used by `compareScenarios` and the menu scores).
- `runRothScenario` projects from the earliest early year with
  `ProjectionOptions.earlyConversions`, so `projectScenario` converts each in
  its own pre-plan year with the normal cascade and tax (step 5b withholds
  under `assets`). The rows are then trimmed back to the normal start, which
  match a normal run from there on. The trimmed years add only their tax
  minus the same years with no conversion, as early taxes.
- `activeEarlyConversions` skips an entry when the amount isn't positive, the
  year is at or after the projection start, or the year is before
  `refs.asOfDate`'s year (balances entered today already include it).
- UI: the Conversion step's per-year list shows the years from today to the
  start first, with a note of the extra tax. Editing them never switches the
  strategy to manual, and the scheduled total covers only the strategy's
  years. Plan details lists each as its own row.
- IRMAA lookback doesn't see the trimmed years (matters only if someone is
  63 or older at the conversion).

## Menu

`ConversionStrategyMenu` sits above the plan Timeline on desktop, and in the
mobile Plan details modal (not in the Conversion wizard step). Manual is first. Each row shows **Total impact**
from `scoreConversionStrategies`: one shared no-conversion baseline, then one
Roth run per option. Formula lives in `totalImpact()` (same as the results
header): inheritance + tax savings + Medicare Part B. After-tax assets at
RMD are excluded (same estate snapshot as inheritance, just earlier).
Choosing Manual with no saved years copies the current live schedule
so the year grid has numbers to edit. Each row has a "?" tooltip
(`STRATEGY_HELP` in `labels.ts`).

Fill-bracket is one row that reads Fill {12% / 22% / 24%} bracket. The dollar
is the selected rate; all three rates are precomputed.

## Persistence

- Missing optimizer or unknown strategy → `manual`.
- `manual` with no schedule stays `manual` (engine spreads convertible evenly).
- Explicit `manualSchedule` stays `manual`.
- Missing `targetBracketRate` on `fillBracket` → `0.22`.
- `earlyConversions` keeps only entries with a finite year and a positive
  amount, one per year; an empty list is dropped.

## Gotchas

- `minTax` runs a few hundred projections (up to about 150ms). `minTaxSchedule`
  keeps a one-entry cache keyed on the household minus `optimizer`, so the
  menu and the results share one search. Its schedule ignores every
  `optimizer` field, including `convertAmount`.
- `minTax` targets the results' Lifetime taxes number, so it never shows more
  lifetime tax than no conversion or any other strategy. It does not count the
  tax heirs will owe on a leftover tax-deferred balance, so on a plan with
  steady income and a short RMD window it may convert little or nothing.

- IRMAA uses baseline income only (one pass). Later-year balances may be
  lower; the engine caps. Fill-bracket re-projects with its own schedule
  (see the table above).
- Brackets and IRMAA floors are not inflation-adjusted in the projection.
- `allowOverConvertible` and `convertAmount` apply to even / immediate /
  manual, not to the computed strategies.
- Editing a year in the Conversion wizard copies the live schedule into
  `manual` and does not keep a computed strategy live.

## Conversions in RMD years

The "Convert in RMD years" switch (`RmdYearConversionsSwitch`, also on the
empty-conversion notice when it would open years up) sets
`optimizer.allowConversionsInRmdYears`. Every strategy then sees the whole
projection as its window. Anything that builds a strategy's household (like
`minTaxSchedule`'s starting points and its cache key) must keep that flag, or
the search silently ignores RMD years.

## Working years and the four windows

"Convert in working years" (`WorkingYearConversionsSwitch`) sets
`optimizer.allowConversionsInWorkingYears`: `strategyStartYear` becomes the
projection start, and turning it on also sets "Projection starts" to this
year when the plan starts later. Off leaves the start alone. With both
switches every strategy's window is one of four: retirement years only,
plus working years, plus RMD years, or all three. Even and the manual
default are a level amount found by bisection (`levelThroughWindow`) so the
money lasts the window instead of front-loading. `conversionWindows.test.ts`
runs every strategy through all four.

